content.thinking:
The `marks()` walker: `if (c.nodeType === 3) continue; if (c.classList.contains("ann-mark"))` — my `El.classList.contains` is fine. But `_wrapQuote` sets `sp.className = "ann-mark"` as a property directly; my El constructor sets `this.className` and builds classList from the constructor arg — setting `.className` afterwards does NOT update the classList set! So `classList.contains("ann-mark")` is false → marks() finds nothing.

Fix the shim: make `className` a setter that rebuilds the set. Let me restructure El to use a private set with a className accessor.

That's a shim fidelity issue. Implement:
```js
class El {
  constructor(tag, cls){ ... this._set = new Set(); this.className = cls || ""; this.classList = {...} }
  set className(v){ this._set = new Set(String(v).split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean)); }
  get className(){ return [...this._set].join(" "); }
}
```
But the constructor assigns `this.className = cls` after `this.classList = ...`; the getter returns "ann-mark" ✓.

Since classList is an object referencing `this._set` via closure, reassigning `_set` on className assignment would break the closure reference. Use a mutable holder: keep `this._set` and have className setter clear+add into the same Set:
```js
set className(v){ this._set.clear(); String(v).split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).forEach(c=>this._set.add(c)); }
```
Good.

Also the test's `marks()` uses `c.classList.contains("ann-mark")` ✓ and `m[0].className === "ann-mark"` ✓.

Order in the constructor: I need `this._set = new Set()` before `this.className = cls`.

